BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Evening With Wright Thompson & Michael Koryta
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261025T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261025T235900
UID:bb129947fdceaf526d339c81e4198a35@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/1265
 14e/2147483647/strip/true/crop/2160x1080+0+0/resize/254x127!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F59%2Fab%2F2ae50658461cb1
 f9972021eada22%2F102526-wright-thompson-photo.png
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Buskirk-Chumley Theater\nBLOOMINGTON BOOK FEST: W
 RIGHT THOMPSON\nJoin the Bloomington Book Festival for the third annual pr
 esentation of the Bob Hammel Writing Award.\n The 2026 winner and keynote 
 speaker is Wright Thompson\, a New York Times-bestselling author and rever
 ed journalist. A native of Clarksdale\, in the Mississippi Delta\, Thompso
 n has spent two decades proving that the best stories are often about ambi
 tion\, loss\, family\, and the price of greatness.\n His latest book\, The
  Barn: The Secret History of a Murder in Mississippi \, is a haunting acco
 unt of the brutal 1955 murder of Emmett Till\, named a Best Book of the Ye
 ar by the Washington Post\, TIME\, and Vanity Fair\, and nominated for a P
 EN America Literary Award.\n In his journalism for ESPN and The Atlantic\,
  Thompson has writen the definitive profiles of legends ranging from Ted W
 illiams to Michael Jordan\, revealed the secret history of Tiger Woods’ 
 Navy SEAL fascination\, and penned many of the most-read articles in ESPN
 ’s history. Some of those pieces were collected in his New York Times be
 stseller\, The Cost of These Dreams: Sports Stories and Other Serious Busi
 ness .\n Another bestseller was Pappyland: A Story of Family\, Fine Bourbo
 n\, and the Things That Last \, which is both a chronicle of the remarkabl
 e history of world-famous Pappy Van Winkle whiskey and a meditation on the
  traditions worth preserving.\n We’re hoping Wright will offer an opinio
 n on Indiana’s finest bourbon by the end of the night\, and we can promi
 se a few rare bottles will be on the auction table.\n Presenting the Hamme
 l Award and joining Wright in conversation is Bloomington native Michael K
 oryta\, a New York Times-bestselling author\, whose new novel\, Die Famous
  \, releases September 22. Set in Indiana in 1937\, the novel follows Sava
 nnah Cody and her son Alford as they pursue the man she once loved and the
  nation now fears – the outlaw Nickel Terhune – from the Midwest to Ma
 ine. Stephen King called Die Famous “the best gangster novel I’ve ever
  read.” The evening with Wright Thompson will be Koryta’s only appeara
 nce in Bloomington this fall. Both writers will sign books following the e
 vent.\n Don’t miss the chance to hear these authors talk about their cra
 ft\, influences\, – and bourbon preferences! While also raising money fo
 r the good work of BloomREADS.\nTickets: https://buskirkchumley.org/events
 /an-evening-with-wright-thompson-michael-koryta-october-25-2026/
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E Kirkwood Ave\, Bloomington\, Indi
 ana 47408
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/an-evening-with-wright-th
 ompson-amp-michael-koryta-19-07-2026-12-59-22
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
